GitHub Pages runs Jekyll over a published directory unless this file is
present. Nothing here is a Jekyll site — the pages are built by
generator/bin/build.dart and uploaded finished — so Jekyll would only be a
second, unasked-for renderer running over the artefact the gate passed.

It is not hypothetical. Jekyll excludes every path beginning with `_` or `.`
from what it publishes. The calculator's own files are safe (`/calc/<hash8>/`),
but any future underscore-prefixed path would silently stop being served, and
Jekyll would also try to read `{{ … }}` and `{% … %}` in HTML it does not
understand. An empty `.nojekyll` at the publishing root turns all of that off
and GitHub Pages serves the files exactly as uploaded.

The build copies this file from assets/root/ into _site/ with the favicon.
generator/bin/verify.dart fails if it is missing from the artefact. See
deploy/RUNBOOK.md § 2.
